# Git — sync `master` first

Before any work that depends on how the repo compares to **`master`**, refresh local `master` from the remote. Do this **before** creating a branch, reporting “ahead/behind”, opening a PR, or running `git diff` / `git log` against `master`.

## Required sequence

Run from the repository root (use full permissions if `git fetch` needs network/SSH):

```bash
git fetch origin master
git checkout master
git pull origin master --ff-only
```

If `pull --ff-only` fails (local `master` diverged), stop and tell the user — do not rebase or reset `master` unless they ask.

Then:

- **New branch:** `git checkout -b <branch-name>` from the updated `master` (or from an explicitly named base they request).
- **Compare to master:** `git log master..HEAD --oneline` and `git diff master...HEAD --stat` (or `master..HEAD` for merge-base semantics as appropriate).
- **PR summary:** Base the description on `master...HEAD` **after** the fetch/pull above.

## Do not

- Compare to stale local `master` without fetching first.
- Create a feature branch without updating `master` when the user asked for work “from master” or “vs master”.
- Force-push `master` or run destructive git on `master` without explicit user request.

## When fetch fails

If `git fetch` / `pull` fails (auth, network), say so clearly, report **local-only** `master` vs branch state, and ask the user to pull on their machine before trusting “up to date with GitHub”.
